Give regtest mainnet's identity: genesis, address prefixes, p2p magic, difficulty.

WHY

The real eigenwallet ASB refuses to build a wallet against our local chain:

    Failed to create new wallet: cannot find agreement block with server

That message is electrum-pool's checkpoint reconciliation. It walks its local
chain back looking for a block the server also has, and a freshly created
wallet's only checkpoint is the genesis block of its configured network. A
mainnet-configured ASB looks for 000000000019d668..., a stock regtest chain
answers with 0f9188f13cb7b2c7..., and there is no agreement at any height. It
is not an explicit network check, but it is a genesis check in effect. Chain
length is not the cause: the error is byte-identical on 101- and 500-block
chains.

We cannot instead tell the ASB it is on regtest — the shipped binary's env
selector only ever reaches Mainnet or Testnet, and validate_config rejects the
mismatch outright ("Expected bitcoin network in config file to be Bitcoin but
was Regtest"). Mainnet is the configuration we WANT under test anyway: it
carries the 10-minute lock-mempool timeout and cancel=24/punish=144 that our
driver hardcodes, where the Regtest env would substitute 30s/100/50.

THE FOUR HUNKS

  genesis      So electrum-pool finds its agreement block. This is the one that
               is load-bearing; the others are consequences.

  prefixes     bech32_hrp "bcrt" -> "bc" (and base58 to mainnet), so every tool
               in the rig speaks the same addresses as the peers and nothing
               has to translate.

  p2p magic    electrs derives BOTH its starting genesis and its p2p magic from
               its --network flag, and only signet's magic is configurable. With
               mainnet genesis on the chain, electrs must run as --network
               bitcoin, which means bitcoind has to speak mainnet magic too.
               Without this hunk electrs dies with
               "missing prev_blockhash: 000000000019d668...".

MINING, AND WHY THE FOURTH HUNK IS NEEDED

Mainnet's genesis carries difficulty-1 proof of work, and nBits is inherited:
GetNextWorkRequired returns pindexLast->nBits between retargets.

fPowAllowMinDifficultyBlocks rescues only the FIRST block. Block 1's timestamp
is ~2026 against genesis's 2009, far more than nPowTargetSpacing*2, so the
min-difficulty rule returns nProofOfWorkLimit. But block 2 is mined
milliseconds after block 1, fails that same test, and takes the else branch,
which walks back past block 1 (whose nBits IS the easy limit) to genesis and
returns 0x1d00ffff. Every block after the first then costs ~2^32 hashes.

This fails almost silently: generatetoaddress exhausts its default maxtries and
returns FEWER blocks than asked without erroring, so a caller that does not
check the height sees success and a 1-block chain.

Hence the pow.cpp hunk: return the proof-of-work limit outright when
fPowNoRetargeting is set. Only regtest sets it, so no other chain is affected.

The merkle root assert is left alone deliberately: every network shares the same
genesis coinbase transaction, so mainnet's merkle root already equals regtest's.

DANGER

A node with mainnet's genesis AND mainnet's magic is wire-compatible with the
real Bitcoin network. Left to its own devices it would happily dial real peers
and start syncing the real chain onto what is supposed to be a private one.
Anything running this build MUST disable outbound networking:

    -connect=0 -dnsseed=0 -bind=127.0.0.1:<port>

The flake's `checks.mining` does exactly that. Do not use this build for
anything that is allowed to reach the internet.

